orker gets exactly one frame: its prompt carries `_role.md` and that frame's packet — paste both in full, or hand the two file paths for the worker to read first (equivalent; the worker starts from exactly those two documents either way) — plus a dispatch context with `PROJECT_DIR`, `frame_id`, whether the frame has a **confirmed sketch** on disk (the worker dresses that layout rather than redrawing it — frame-worker core § When a confirmed sketch exists), canvas size, and caption status + keep-out band if captions are enabled.\n193:**Full-bleed backgrounds ride on a `class=\"clip\"` layer, never the `#root`.** A frame's ground (color field / gradient / grid) is its own full-duration background clip — a `background` set on the `#root` / `data-composition-id` element is clip-gated to the frame's window and is not a dependable ground, so dark content can land on the black host `body` and render invisible. The video's base ground is painted by the assembler from `frame.md`'s `canvas` color onto the index `#root`. (Full rule + self-check: `../hyperframes/references/frame-worker-core.md`.)\n197:After audio timings exist, build captions in the background and assemble the index:\n199:`node <SKILL_DIR>/scripts/captions.mjs build --storyboard ./STORYBOARD.md --audio-meta ./audio_meta.json --hyperframes . --out ./caption_groups.json &`\n203:`captions.mjs` uses the project's `.hyperframes/caption-skin.html` (copied in Step 2) as the caption look, injecting brand tokens from `frame.md`; with no skin present it renders the built-in default pill. `captions: skipped (<reason>)` is valid. Continue without captions when explicitly skipped.\n205:**Gate:** every frame is marked `animated` (collaborative: the sketch sheet was confirmed at Step 4), `index.html` exists, and captions are built or explicitly skipped.\n211:Goal: Verify the assembled video, get user approval, and render the final MP4.\n213:Inject transitions, run checks, pause for review, then render.\n225:`snapshot` stitches the captured frames into one contact sheet (`snapshots/contact-sheet.jpg`). Inspect the midpoint frames for layout failures, then compare the two images around every cut. A continuing element must keep the promised position, scale, opacity, and direction; fix any visible pop before rendering.\n229:After checks pass, pause for user review — the review loop's final look (`../hyperframes/references/review-loop.md` § 4): one question, on the final Studio preview — render now, or what changes? (Autonomous: the same, as the one kept question — open the preview even though the user didn't ask for one.) Then deliver the MP4 with the contact sheet and the frame ids so revisions can target a single frame.\n233:Render only after user approval (autonomous mode: after the render-or-changes question):\n235:`npx hyperframes render --skill=product-launch-video --quality high --output renders/video.mp4`\n237:Do not rerun `lint`, `check`, or `snapshot` after rendering unless the user asks.\n239:**Gate:** `lint` and `check` passed and the snapshots were inspected before render; user approved at the review pause (autonomous: checks passed and the delivery includes the contact sheet); `renders/video.mp4` exists. Final reply states MP4 path and final duration.\n247:**Background scripts:** the workflow ships only these scripts under `scripts/`: `build-frame` for adopting + brand-remixing a frame preset into `frame.md` (+ caption skin); `audio` for TTS, transcription, BGM, SFX, and duration syncing; `captions`; `transitions` for inject and verify; `stage-assets` for copying frame-named assets into `assets/`; and `assemble-index`. Everything else is handled by the `hyperframes` CLI.","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e67314b9-7a06-4fce-ab28-44720e34a69c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"22d95c73-257c-40cc-8cbf-1a701c185d79","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"environment","snapshot":{"workingDirectory":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste","isWorktree":false,"isGitRepo":true,"additionalWorkingDirectories":["/home/diogenes/.t3/userdata/attachments"],"platform":"linux","shell":"bash","osVersion":"Linux 6.19.14-108.fc42.x86_64"},"changes":[{"field":"workingDirectory","from":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"442a1ae7-8ef5-434e-ae57-d00624178134","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:21:30.860Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n# Environment update\n - Primary working directory: /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste (was /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour)\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"442a1ae7-8ef5-434e-ae57-d00624178134","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuaC5TcGThPPUnCdch6V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The elaborate product-launch-video workflow doesn't quite fit here since I already have real recordings, fal TTS narration, and a script — I've deviated from the standard pipeline. I'll put together my own monolithic approach to assembling a finished video that shows off baste's capabilities and why someone would use it.\n\nOn rendering: the skill says to wait for approval, but the user's direct request was to get the demo video done showing full capabilities. Since rendering locally is a reversible, non-outward-facing action, I think proceeding to render and then reporting back with options for changes is the right call here — the explicit user request outweighs the skill's general gating preference.\n\nNow I'm sketching the composition scene by scene using timing.json (total ~69.3s at 1920×1080). For scene one, 0-8.7s, I'm thinking through a title sequence on pattern paper: the Baste wordmark fading in small top-left, then a demographic \"persona card\" styled in Plex Mono showing \"users aged 25-34\" struck through with a red thread line, transitioning into a bold Bodoni headline reading \"A person does\" as the voiceover delivers the line about interfaces being cut for narrow age brackets.\n\nFor syncing text beats to the narration audio, I'm checking whether the narration JSON files include word-level timing data, and if not, I'm considering using ffmpeg's silencedetect to find pauses at phrase boundaries as a deterministic substitute for actual transcription timing.\n\nFor the second measure (8.733–19.267s), covering \"Baste starts with one person,\" I'm planning the visual as three companion portraits with focus shifting to Ren, surrounded by tags flying in for films, music genres, favorite places, and dislikes — all pulled from the real persona JSON data to build a tailor's measuring-tape aesthetic.\n\nFor the third measure, \"fitting\" (19.267–27.367s, 8.1s long), I'm checking the footage timing against the scene length — the fitting.webm clip runs 13.19s but I only have 8.1s, so I need to figure out whether HyperFrames supports a playback rate attribute to speed up the clip, or whether I should just trim it to capture the key tab-click moments showing all three personas.\n\nRather than compute exact frame timings, maybe I should check if HyperFrames supports a playback rate attribute, or simply extend the scene durations in timing.json so footage has room to breathe after each voice line—since the total video length of ~80s still fits within the 90s brief.\n\nSince narration audio files are separate per scene, I can place each one at whatever start time I want, so I'll build my own scene plan sized to both voice length and footage needs: giving the \"why\" scene about 8.7s for its 7.52s line, \"measure\" roughly 10.6s, \"fitting\" around 11s to cover several tab switches, and \"baste\" sized around its 8.73s line plus the archive-filling footage.\n\nFor the remaining scenes, I'm mapping each one to the right footage segment -- trimming or speeding up the archive replay where needed, pairing \"fit\" with the method clip's develop-and-judge portion, pairing \"cut\" with the archive's \"cut\" segment plus the tokens clip, and lining up \"talk\" with its own clip start.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the overall story order to best convey what it's like to use this and why someone would: opening with the why, then measure, fitting room, baste with the studio archive replay, fit with the method contact sheet and judge weights, and finally cut.\n\nFor the cut segment, I want to show the archive footage from provider selection through the \"Cut the assets\" click and the map filling in, which runs about 26 seconds total — too long, so I'm thinking about trimming to show the provider selection and click (around 7.4 s) since the fal generation was emphasized, even though timing the cut precisely is tricky.\n\nI'm also considering splitting the archive clip into two speed-adjusted segments to compress the full 26 s down, though I need to check whether playback-rate control is supported before committing. For framing, the Studio viewport is 16:10 while the output frame is 16:9, so I'm weighing whether to crop the footage to fill 1920×1080 or present it inside a browser-style device frame on the pattern paper background instead.\n\nGiven the window-card approach leaves too little room for captions below 960px tall, I'm leaning toward burning captions directly over the footage as a dark pill near the bottom, and I'm also thinking about adding simulated camera moves, like zooming into specific regions of the archive map footage.\n\nFor the camera-move implementation, I need to use a non-timed wrapper with GSAP since the timed video element itself can't have its dimensions animated directly, and crucially a video with data-start can never have an ancestor that also carries data-start. So I'm working out the structure: if timing lives only on the wrapper section, does the video inside still get synced correctly without its own data-start, or does the runtime need that attribute directly on the video to know playback timing?\n\nI should check data-attributes.md and creator-editing-recipes.md for how trim/offset attributes like data-media-start work alongside the camera recipes.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3252,"cache_read_input_tokens":151824,"output_tokens":4124,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3988},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3252,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4124,"cache_read_input_tokens":151824,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3252,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3252},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":45217,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"hyperframes-core","type":"assistant","uuid":"a9369a3d-5bcd-401b-adc7-c1eb5ba1ecc5","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:22:21.273Z","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
