 \"fal-ai/elevenlabs/tts/multilingual-v2\",\n  \"voice\": \"Aria\",\n  \"text\": \"Most interfaces are cut for “users aged twenty-five to thirty-four.” Nobody in that bracket has taste. A person does.\",\n  \"requestId\": \"01a1276e-3054-7341-883e-45115b241af6\",\n  \"generatedAt\": \"2026-10-10T20:08:17.334Z\"\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b4a72831-b597-40c6-8127-f29559e14e0d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"bd8eb773-56fb-4bad-89fd-495cf5200589","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"environment","snapshot":{"workingDirectory":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","isWorktree":false,"isGitRepo":true,"additionalWorkingDirectories":["/home/diogenes/.t3/userdata/attachments"],"platform":"linux","shell":"bash","osVersion":"Linux 6.19.14-108.fc42.x86_64"},"changes":[{"field":"workingDirectory","from":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0cd9d9b8-c2a9-483c-bf64-86861cfdbcc9","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:22:50.333Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n# Environment update\n - Primary working directory: /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour (was /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste)\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"0cd9d9b8-c2a9-483c-bf64-86861cfdbcc9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuaJaZEpiktFmWfp5jfF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm aligning the phrase boundaries with the caption groups for the \"why\" section, shifting timestamps by the global offset to get the voice-synced ranges.\n\nFor \"measure,\" I'm mapping the five lines about rewatched films, music, rooms, and app dislikes to their shifted timestamps starting at 9.183.\n\nFor \"fitting,\" I'm timing how the interface adapts—layout, type, colour, motion—across the four lines, offset by 19.717.\n\nFor \"baste,\" I'm timing the explanation of trying loose versions and having a vision judge evaluate them, offset by 27.817.\n\nThe middle segment covering \"warm and cool, sparse and dense\" is long, so I'm trying to split it proportionally by character count since there's no clean silence detected—estimating the split around 32.18 seconds globally, though I want to rerun silence detection with a lower threshold to pin it down more precisely.\n\nMoving to the \"fit\" segment, I'm mapping out its three phrases with their global timestamps, then doing the same for \"cut,\" breaking it into five pieces covering images, vector icons, ambient loops, and design tokens, before starting on the \"talk\" segment's timing.\n\nFinishing \"talk\" with its phrases about asking the persona questions, then timing out the final \"yours\" segment with two lines about bringing your own people and measuring them in the Studio. I'm noticing the \"yours\" scene runs only about 5.4 seconds, which feels short for an ending card, so I want to reconsider the pacing there.\n\nNext I'm thinking about extending the ending with a few extra seconds to hold on an end card showing the logo and command, bringing the total runtime to roughly 72.5 seconds -- shorter than the 90-second target but still acceptable. Then I shift to the visual design: the video is 1920x1080 with 1440x900 footage, using the site's established palette and typefaces, which means I'll need font-face declarations pointing to locally shipped font files, so I'm checking where those live in the project.\n\nI'm sketching a \"tailor's atelier\" concept where each scene shows a kicker label, a Bodoni headline, and the footage presented like a framed garment on pattern paper, with burned-in captions. I'm now working out how to scale the 1440x900 footage within the 1920x1080 frame so there's enough margin left for caption space without overlaying the footage itself.\n\nI'm reconsidering the frame placement so burned captions sit below the footage without overlapping UI content, leaving around 110px at the bottom for text, and starting to sketch the canvas background with a subtle grid pattern on warm paper.\n\nFor the layout itself, I'm centering the 1440x900 footage window with rounded corners and a soft shadow, then thinking about using the left margin for a vertical tailor's tag label and the right margin for a tape-measure-style progress indicator with cm ticks that fills as time advances.\n\nA static screen recording would feel flat, so I'm planning subtle motion for each scene — a gentle push-in on the footage window plus punch-zooms toward key regions like the archive map. I'm sketching out the first scene as a title card on pattern paper with no footage, just bold type to establish the \"why\" of the piece.\n\nScene two introduces the three portraits—Moss, Ren, and Ada—as pinned measurement cards styled like tailor's tape, revealing real persona data for films, music, spaces, and pet peeves as the narration lists them. I want to pull actual details from the personas JSON files rather than inventing placeholder content.\n\nFor scene three, the fitting sequence runs from 19.267 to 27.367 seconds using footage from fitting.webm, trimmed from source timestamp 2.88 to 16.07. I'm working out approximate timing for cursor movement and tab clicks—moving to tab one around 4.9s, tab two around 8.0s, back to tab zero around 11.0s—to fit the 8.1-second scene duration.\n\nI'm considering trimming the start point and using a slight playback speedup around 1.3-1.5x to compress the 13-second source clip into the available scene time without the cursor movements looking too unnatural. Another option is adjusting the narration pacing to add breathing room between scenes, which would let the total video run a bit longer than 69 seconds if needed to accommodate the footage properly.\n\nFor the baste scene, I'm examining the timestamps in archive.webm to locate the key moments — selecting the fal provider, running the process, and seeing candidates land in the archive map — so I can isolate the most visually interesting 19-second segment where results populate.\n\nI'm also planning to speed up that archive-filling segment to roughly 10 seconds, since it's just a progress animation and the cursor stays mostly still, and I want to add a zoom overlay on the archive map's grid region based on its position in the contact sheet.\n\nFor overlay text, I'm thinking a minimal kicker like \"No. 04 — Baste\" with a small counter, keeping it light since the captions do the narrative work. For Scene 5, covering \"fit\" (37.3–45.033s), I'm pairing the line about keeping the best in each region rather than one winner with method.webm footage showing the contact sheet developing, judge scores, and grease-pencil loops marking kept drafts — this visual matches the idea of showing a range of options rather than a single result.\n\nThe timing math is tricky: using the method clip from 2.8 to 16.71s is 13.9s of source footage, which at a 1.8x playback rate gives 7.7s — too fast for a scroll-driven feel, so a 9s scroll at 1.8x would only cover 5s. I'm considering trimming to just the judging and cut portion, roughly 6 to 16.71s, at a 1.4x rate to land closer to 7.6s.\n\nAlternatively, I could show the archive map with final kept cells and overlay a \"judge weights\" graphic — a tailor's-ticket card sliding in from the right showing persona alignment, visual quality, uniqueness, coherence, and usability scores, tying into the brief's Story #5 about the judge's weighting system.\n\nMoving into Scene 6, covering \"cut\" from 45.033 to 54.933s (9.9s), the line is about the finished outputs being ready to use — images, vector icons, ambient loops, and design tokens for CSS.\n\nWriting scene timing breakdown...\n\nVideos need muted and playsinline attributes, with loop length around 5s matching the seedance footage timing.\n\nFor Scene 7, the \"talk\" segment runs 54.933 to 63.9 seconds (8.97s), using talk.webm footage with specific start/end markers at 3.25s, 11.63s, and 16.24s.\n\nLooking at the full interaction sequence—clicking the Ren tab, waiting, clicking a suggestion, response bubbles with typing delays, another click, another response—the raw duration comes to 13s against the 8.97s scene budget, needing a 1.45x playback rate to fit. I'm considering cropping instead: trimming the start to around 4.0s (just before the Ren tab click) or even skipping that click entirely and starting around 5.6s when Ren is already selected, right before the suggestion click, to avoid speeding up the chat reading too much.\n\nFor scene 8 (yours.webm), I'm planning a zoom into the chat panel for legibility, then transitioning to an end card with the Baste wordmark, tagline, and install command. I need to check the README or site config for the actual deployed domain URL to include, and I'm extending the scene length to around 73.5s total to give the end card enough hold time, while also considering whether background music is needed.\n\nNarration alone should work without added music or SFX. For captions, I'll build timed div elements for the clips and also generate a WebVTT file for the site. Between scenes, I'm planning simple GSAP crossfades, including a stitched red \"thread\" wipe effect for a tasteful transition.\n\nFor the footage scenes, I want a persistent window frame element that doesn't jump between scenes, with the videos crossfading inside it. The frame itself can be a non-timed wrapper controlled by opacity tweens, while each video inside keeps its own data-start and duration as a proper clip.\n\nFor the actual crossfades, I'll wrap each video in its own non-timed `.inner` div and tween that wrapper's opacity instead of touching the clip directly, since clips are always visible outside their window regardless of opacity. The same wrapper can also handle zoom/punch effects via scale and position tweens, leaving the question of whether the outer frame itself should be timed or fully persistent.\n\nThat's fine since fromTo defaults to immediateRender at time 0 in a paused timeline. Actually, maybe cleaner to keep scene sections as timed clips without videos, placing the videos as sibling timed clips layered absolutely over the frame chrome, with a non-timed viewport wrapper clipping each to the frame bounds for the zoom/crossfade effect.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the frame chrome itself—maybe a paper-card border with shadow and tape accents at the corners, plus a small label beneath the frame like a recording caption, though I don't want to overload it with detail. I'm also considering whether each scene needs a kicker label above the frame given the 1440px dimensions.\n\nReconsidering the layout, I think a left rail could work better: the main frame sits at scaled proportions within the canvas, leaving a 320px sidebar for chapter metadata—a mono \"No. 03\" marker, a Bodoni-style chapter title, and a brief descriptor line. This tailor's-ticket motif would aid comprehension during muted autoplay, though I need to watch for redundancy with the caption band below.\n\nI considered an alternative where the chapter label hangs off the frame's corner like a garment tag, but that feels unnecessarily complex compared to the editorial left-rail approach. I'm settling on the left rail with the frame positioned to leave margin on both sides, captions in a band beneath spanning the frame's width, and I'm confirming the pixel math works out so the UI stays legible at the scale the site will actually display it.\n\nSince UI text will shrink to near-illegibility once displayed at typical web widths, I'm planning zoom/punch-ins on key regions like the archive map, chat, and tokens code so those details stay readable. For rendering, I'm thinking through the headless Chrome render time at 30fps for a 70-second clip — probably manageable within 5-15 minutes — while also weighing that the skill calls for user approval before rendering, though the user's repeated \"continue\" signals may count as that approval already.\n\nRendering is just local file generation, not something hard to reverse, so given the user's explicit instruction to produce full demonstrations, I'll go ahead and render and publish locally without deploying or pushing anything. Before that, I need to gather the supporting data: the personas JSON for the measure scene, local font files for Bodoni Moda, Instrument Sans, and IBM Plex Mono, companion portraits, the fittings.json for scores, and the logo SVG component.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68590,"cache_read_input_tokens":91599,"output_tokens":8974,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":8675},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68590,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8974,"cache_read_input_tokens":91599,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68590,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68590},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thin