{"parentUuid":"fe672574-7ae9-44a7-a1d2-f7ff37cb46a4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuauZe9UnUpTXHfW5CNu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The footage works well, so now I'm mapping out the composition timing from the timing.json file across the full 69.33 seconds, breaking it into sections like \"why\" (0–8.733s) with specific voice phrases and timestamps, followed by \"measure\" starting at 8.733s.\n\nContinuing through: \"fitting\" (19.267–27.367s) covering how the interface adapts per person, \"baste\" (27.367–37.3s) describing the process of generating loose design versions judged for quality, \"fit\" (37.3–45.033s) explaining how the best options from each region are preserved rather than picking one winner, and \"cut\" (45.033–54.933s) starting with the final usable output.\n\nI'm noting the narration totals 69.3s against the 90s brief, so I'll extend the end card with a ~3s hold on the URL/command to bring the total to roughly 72s. Now I'm sketching the visual plan in the house style — pattern paper background, ink and thread-red accents, chalk blue, Bodoni Moda for display text, Instrument Sans for body, IBM Plex Mono for labels — and starting to define the frame size.\n\nI'm also working through how to fit the 1440×900 footage into the 1920×1080 canvas without awkward cropping, weighing options like scaling it into a bordered \"screen\" window with margins, filling the frame and cropping top/bottom (which risks cutting the nav), or presenting it inside a browser-style window sized to leave room for a caption strip at the bottom.\n\nModest 1.3x zooms stay crisp enough, so I'll mix footage at scale 1.0–1.2 with slow Ken Burns-style pushes rather than static 1:1 frames. For captions, instead of using the skill's built-in caption pipeline, I'll build my own: one caption per phrase timed to silence-detected boundaries, placed in Instrument Sans 30px ink-on-paper styling in the band below the footage window.\n\nI also need to export a matching VTT file for the site player. Now I'm sketching the opening scene: a mock demographic persona card gets struck through with red thread as \"Nobody in that bracket has taste\" appears, replaced by \"A person does\" in bold Bodoni italic, all set against the drafting grid paper background.\n\nI'm debating whether to let the three portraits slide in right at that line or hold them for the next scene — I think it's cleaner to keep scene one purely typographic and save the portraits for the \"measure\" scene that follows.\n\nFor scene 2, I'm picturing Ren's portrait styled like a measurement sheet, labeled as a profile card, with measurement lines unfurling tailor's-tape style beside it as each phrase plays: films they rewatch, music they work to, the spaces they feel at home in, and the things that annoy them in an app, each rendered as a labeled row with specific example entries.\n\nI want to also tease Moss and Ada as small thumbnails in a \"wardrobe\" strip at the bottom, subtly setting up the three-persona idea for scene 3 without undercutting the \"one person\" narration. I need to pull Ren's actual data — films, music, spaces, pet peeves — from the base-personas source file.\n\nMoving to scene 3, the fitting scene runs from 19.27 to 27.37 using the fitting footage clip, trimmed between 2.88 and 16.07 seconds.\n\nFor the tab switches within the fitting footage, I'm noting the detected clicks at roughly 6.48 (Ren), 10.24 (Ada), and 14.08 (Moss), spaced about 3.8 seconds apart. The narration here runs about 8 seconds, but my trimmed footage is 13.2 seconds — too long — so I'm weighing options like speeding playback to 1.5x (bringing it to about 8.8s) or trimming the start closer to the first click to better match the narration timing.\n\nI settle on taking the segment from 5.2 to 15.9 seconds and playing it at roughly 1.32x speed, which compresses it to 8.1 seconds and aligns the three transitions nicely with the narration beats — Ren appearing as the line \"Then it fits an interface to them\" plays, Ada lining up with \"Same screen, three people,\" and Moss landing near \"the motion all change.\" I'm checking whether a 1.32x speed-up feels too noticeable, but concluding it should read fine since the cursor and transition animations just feel slightly brisker.\n\nNow I'm thinking through scene 4, the baste scene running 27.37 to 37.3 seconds, where the narration talks about trying dozens of loose versions and a vision judge scoring each one. I'm weighing whether to use the method.webm footage of the contact sheet developing and being judged (source 2.8 to 12.1, giving about 9.3-9.8 seconds at normal speed, close to the scene's 9.93s length) versus the archive clip that visually shows the \"warm and cool, sparse and dense\" axis labels, since that pairs more literally with the narration wording.\n\nFor scene 5, fit (37.3–45.03), the narration about keeping the best in each region rather than one winner maps well to the archive map clip with cells filling in per-region bests and a \"3 directions kept · best 0.92\" counter, before transitioning into scene 6's cut and tokens footage for \"ready to use\" assets. I'm pinpointing that within the method clip, the judge phase with loupe and criteria bars kicks in around source 12.1, and aligning the narration timestamp for \"a vision judge looks at every one\" to a source range of roughly 9.7–11.5 within that clip.\n\nMapping out the contact sheet's progression frame by frame — empty strips filling in row by row across five generations until all 24 drafts are developed, then a loupe appears with scoring criteria and a sample quote scoring 0.93 with \"Kept warm and dense\" — and syncing these frame timestamps to the timeline offset starting at 27.37 plus the source delta from 2.8s.\n\nNow I'm trying to tie the scroll's linear progress curve to specific judging and cutting phase thresholds, calculating that the judge phase begins around source time 10.34 and roughly lines up with the voiceover line \"a vision judge looks at every one\" spanning 34.25–36.08. I'm extending this mapping into the next scene about \"fit,\" estimating the cut-phase source time near 12.1–16.7 would align with the loupe showing kept drafts and quotes around 37.3 seconds.\n\nThen I consider whether to continue using that method-page cut clip for the first half of scene 5, or switch to the Studio archive map to show the 5×5 grid filling with candidates for \"the real range of options\" line. I weigh the risk of mixing two UIs in one scene against the narrative benefit of pairing the explanatory method page with the actual product view, and start sketching an alternative where scene 4 instead uses the Studio archive clip — unticking Dry run and running Cut so the archive map populates along the cool/warm, sparse/dense axes, which maps nicely onto both the axis-label line and the judge log entries like \"Candidate 13 (generation 3) judged 0.92.\"\n\nThe fill phase in that archive clip runs about 19 seconds, too long for the 9.9s scene, so I'm testing a 2x playback rate, which still reads as \"dozens\" of candidates since the cursor barely moves during that portion. I'm also reworking the earlier setup segment (provider select, untick Dry run, click Cut) since it's too long even sped up and looks frantic, so I'm considering trimming to start right before the Cut click instead.\n\nThen I realize a single video element can't mix rates without a rate automation lane in the data-automation schema, which does support speed ramps with interpolated points over time — though I'm unsure if the timeline is clip-local. A simpler alternative is splitting into two video elements of the same source, each with its own constant rate, to fake a speed ramp.\n\nWorking through scene 4's timing (27.367–37.3s), I'm placing clip A at 1x covering the \"Cut the assets\" click, trying to pin down the exact source timestamp of the mouse-down/up event from the recorded move/wait/click sequence so clip A ends right as the click completes.\n\nFor clip B (29.0→37.3 timeline, src 11.88→30.98 at 2.3x), I'm checking that the archive map's scroll transition at src ~13.1 still plays smoothly at that speed, and noting that the frame at src 25 shows the archive map with only a few cells filled in the top row, which I need to reconcile with the sparse/dense axis labeling.\n\nThen I'm mapping the narration beats to source timecodes: \"warm and cool, sparse and dense\" lands at src 17.2–23.5 where the map is actively filling, and \"a vision judge looks at every one\" lands at src 23.95–28.15 where log lines show candidates being scored. I'm thinking about adding chalk-blue annotation overlays that point to relevant map regions in sync with these narration cues.\n\nI need to be careful about overlay positioning though, since the page scrolls at certain points. Scroll events occur around src 13.1–13.8 and again near 30.9, but between those points the layout stays stable, so I can lock the grid position for overlays within that stable window.\n\nI'm mapping out the grid coordinates and axis labels precisely within that 1440×900 frame, then considering zooming the footage toward the map region to make it prominent while the \"warm and cool, sparse and dense\" line plays.\n\nReconsidering the overall framing instead: rather than filling the full 1920×1080 canvas, I like presenting the footage as a floating browser-style window on patterned paper with a soft shadow, captions placed below, keeping it closer to a screen-recording feel than a full-bleed video.\n\nNow for Scene 5, spanning roughly 37.3 to 45.03 seconds with the line about keeping the best in each region rather than one winner, I'm weighing whether to continue the archive footage from the point it left off around 30.98 seconds.\n\nAlternatively, the method.webm cut phase works well here: red loops circling kept drafts in the contact sheet, the \"8 kept from 24 drafts\" counter, and the loupe showing a judge's quote. The loops touching multiple drafts across different rows visually reinforce \"not one winner,\" and the line about seeing the real range of options could pair with a composed view showing the spread of surviving picks.\n\nFor scene 5, I'm thinking the first 3.4s uses method.webm from 12.1 to 15.5, where loops land on kept drafts as the loupe pops in, timed to \"It keeps the best in each region, not one winner.\"\n\nThen I'm considering a designed \"range of options\" spread: Moss's 8 kept directions arranged by temperature/density as a big grid of real images with small mono labels like \"warm · dense · 0.93,\" pulling from the method page's data. I need to check which persona's run the method footage actually shows — the tabs default to Moss, so that's likely what's rendered, while the Studio archive clip belongs to Ren.\n\nNow I'm mapping out Scene 6, a 9.9s cut covering \"What comes out is ready to use\" — the archive footage scrolls from the finished state to \"The cut · 5 assets\" showing hero, background, ambient loop, and icon assets with scores and download links, timed against specific source and timeline marks. Then I'm planning the asset wall visuals for \"images,\" \"vector icons,\" and \"ambient loops\" as the line continues.\n\nI'm tracking real generated assets appearing synced to each word: three persona hero images (Moss, Ren, Ada) sliding in, icon SVG collections appearing, and looping video elements starting to play, each with their own timing offsets. For \"and design tokens for CSS, Tailwind or Panda,\" I'm working out the tokens.webm clip timing, including when the Tailwind option gets clicked relative to the scene change list.\n\nI'm mapping the token sheet's frame sequence to figure out when CSS variables, Tailwind, and Panda selections appear and swap in the recording, estimating Tailwind gets clicked around 5.5s and Panda around 8.0s, so I can pick the right clip window to match the narration's 3-second span covering CSS, Tailwind, and Panda in order.\n\nSwitching back to CSS near the end, or ending slightly earlier for cleaner timing—I'll work that out later. I'm also considering zooming into the code pane for legibility, since the design tokens panel sits on the left side.\n\nNow I'm mapping out scene 7, the \"talk\" segment running from 54.93 to 63.9 seconds, syncing the narration lines about asking the persona questions and getting answers in their own words to the specific timestamps in the talk.webm clip.\n\nI'm tracing the clicks within the footage: switching to Ren's tab around 4.4s, clicking \"What are you into?\" at 6.3s with the reply landing between 7.5-10.5s, then clicking \"Why these colours?\" at 12.5s with the palette reply around 13.5-16.2s, trying to work out the source-to-output playback rate.\n\nMapping narration timestamps to source footage—the line about \"what they love\" at 59.38s aligns with source around 9.95s showing Ren's reply about films and music, while \"what would make a design feel like theirs\" around 60.48-62.79s maps to the second question and palette swatches appearing around 11.55-14.9s in source. I'm also weighing whether to trim the tab-switching click entirely to tighten the pacing.\n\nMapping the click on \"What are you into?\" at ~8.0s in source to the scaled timeline, that lands around 56.8s where the narration mentions asking the persona why.\n\nFor the chat column zoom, I'll keep the camera move restrained, maybe a slight push toward the right side where the conversation sits (x 615–1308), scaling around 1.15.\n\nFor scene 8 (\"yours\"), running 63.9–73s, I'm mapping the lines about bringing your own people and measuring them in the Studio to the yours.webm footage, where typing happens around 4.3–5.6s and the summary appears around 6.5–9.5s before the clip ends at 15.77s.\n\nThen I'm planning the end card from 67.9 to 73s: the Baste wordmark in Bodoni italic, the tagline \"Design for the person,\" the CLI command, and the site URL — though I still need to confirm the actual domain by checking the deploy configs. I'm also weighing whether to generate a subtle ambient background track using one of the fal audio models to round out the full 73-second video.\n\nI'm also unsure of other possible endpoint names and considering querying fal's public model search to confirm the right one. I'll aim for a music bed around 75 seconds, warm and minimal in character.\n\nFor captions, I'm thinking burned-in text shown one phrase at a time in the bottom band, styled in Instrument Sans at 30px with the ink color against the paper background. Since the title cards already display the narration as large typography, I want to avoid duplicating captions there while keeping burned-in captions for accessibility on muted autoplay scenes, with a separate VTT file for standard accessibility needs.\n\nNow I'm working out the layout math: footage window at x=240, y=40 sized 1440×900, with the caption band centered around y≈1010. I'll add a mono step label in the left margin, like \"03/08 FITTING,\" to give structural orientation, leaving the right margin empty.\n\nI considered scaling to 1536×960 but decided to keep the window at 1:1 scale for crispness. For transitions, I'm planning the paper background to stay fixed while the footage window content cross-dissolves between scenes — title fades into the measurement sheet, which then gives way to the footage window scaling up from 0.96 with a fade, and quick 0.35s crossfades between subsequent footage scenes.\n\nFor determinism I'll keep all GSAP tweens on a paused timeline with no randomness. For the measurement sheet scene, I'm weighing whether to feature just Ren's portrait with fields like films, music, spaces, and pet peeves, or to show all three personas as columns per the script's \"three portraits\" framing, since the narration describes Baste starting with one person's tastes before expanding.\n\nI'll settle on Ren as the main subject with the four measurements appearing one by one, synced to narration phrases for tightness and legibility. For scene 1's \"why\" section, I'm designing a bland generic persona card (gray template style with a silhouette avatar, age range, and generic interests) that gets struck through diagonally by red basting thread as the narration argues nobody in that demographic bracket actually has taste.\n\nAs the card fades back, a big Bodoni line reads \"A person does.\" with \"person\" set in italic, echoing the house style phrase \"24 drafts for one person,\" before transitioning into the measurement sheet. I'm also considering opening with a small Baste wordmark that appears briefly then shrinks to sit quietly in the top-left margin throughout scene 1 and the end card.\n\nFor the logo, I want to reuse the existing SVG needle-and-wordmark component from the layout folder rather than redraw it. For scene 5's fit sequence, I'm checking the runs JSON for the cyberbotanist example to see the kept candidates' metadata and corresponding thumbnail sizes, thinking through how to lay out a spread of eight kept images for Moss across a temperature-by-density grid with labels like \"cool · sparse · 0.84.\"\n\nI'm leaning toward a designed 4x2 grid titled \"MOSS · 8 DIRECTIONS KEPT FROM 24\" with captions showing each cell's temperature/density words and scores, staggered for visual interest, over the 3.4s method clip and 4.3s spread timing. For scene 6, I'm mapping out the cut sequence showing the archive clip transitioning into a designed asset wall — hero images, icon collections, and ambient loops — illustrating what comes out ready to use.\n\nFor the asset wall itself, I'm structuring it as three columns (Moss, Ren, Ada), each with a hero image, an icon collection, and a looping video, revealed in sync with the narration words \"images,\" \"vector icons,\" and \"ambient loops.\" Mono labels like \"HERO · SEEDREAM V4 · 0.92\" give it a grounded, real-model feel.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the Recraft icon SVGs actually render cleanly despite their lower scores, and reviewing hero image dimensions. For scene 7, the \"talk\" clip runs from 5.7 to 16.6 at roughly 1.215x speed, and scene 8's \"yours\" clip (typing \"The Night Potter\" and a summary) runs 3.6 to 9.4 at 1.45x into a 4-second slot, followed by the end card from 67.9 to 73.0 -- I'm weighing whether this footage works well for the \"bring your own people, measure them in the Studio\" beat.\n\nFor the end card, I'm settling on \"Cut something that fits\" in large Bodoni with the Baste logo and the `npx baste gui` command plus the site URL -- I need to track down that URL by checking the deploy configs and README. I'm also debating whether background music is needed since narration-only demos can feel dry, so I want to search fal's endpoints for a suitable quiet bed track, skipping it if nothing fits. For audio mixing, I'm deciding between placing the individual narration clips at their voiceStart timestamps as separate elements versus concatenating everything into a single narration track with ffmpeg's delay filter.\n\nI'll go with separate audio elements since they stay editable in Studio — eight tracks keyed to voice IDs with start times, durations, and volume set, plus a music bed at low volume with an optional fade-out automation lane. The footage videos need the muted attribute since Playwright recordings have no audio track anyway, and I'm confirming the render pipeline handles webm VP9 at 1440x900 25fps fine through ffmpeg. For HyperFrames structure, I'm working through the rule that timed media elements shouldn't be animated directly -- instead I need a non-timed wrapper div holding the video element, with the video carrying its own start, duration, media-start, and playback-rate data attributes.\n\nThe wrapper itself can't carry data-start since no clip element should have an ancestor that also has data-start, so I'm figuring out how to keep the window frame visible persistently while the runtime shows/hides the actual timed video clips inside it. I'm also confirming that opacity tweens on the .clip element itself are permitted for crossfades between consecutive videos, just not autoAlpha or visibility toggles, since opacity isn't considered a dimension change.\n\nFor the HTML scene sections, I'll mark them with class=\"clip\" alongside data-start/data-duration, with inner elements animated via GSAP against global timeline values, and images can simply live inside clips without special handling. I'm also noting that fonts need in-file @font-face declarations pointing to local woff2 files in the assets/fonts directory to satisfy the lint rules.\n\nFor GSAP, I need to check whether the skeleton index.html pulls the library from a CDN like jsdelivr or has a local copy bundled, since that affects whether rendering needs network access.\n\nFor captions, I'm thinking each phrase should be its own timed clip with data-start/duration inside a caption container, and I should check the hyperframes captions reference docs since captions may have special hard-kill timing rules that differ from regular clips.\n\nI'll also set data-track-index for Studio lane organization, and I'm noting the hyperframes CLI version pinned in package.json for the check command. On rendering, the user has been saying \"continue\" and wants the demo video actually produced and usable on the site, so I'll proceed with rendering since it's local and reversible rather than waiting for explicit per-step approval.\n\nNow I'm working out the exact timing math for the \"cut\" scene transition — calculating when the ease-out curve crosses specific progress thresholds to figure out which source timestamps correspond to the loupe appearing in the scene.\n\nThat gives a method clip range mapping to roughly 37.3–40.7s in the timeline. But this leaves the \"develop\" phase of the method footage (contact sheet developing, src 2.8–12.0) unused, and it's one of the most visually striking parts of the source — I should find a home for it, maybe pairing it with the scene 4 narration about Baste trying dozens of loose versions for a vision judge to evaluate.\n\nActually, this matches scene 4 well: the develop phase could show frames evolving across generations for \"dozens of loose versions,\" brief panel variation lines for \"warm and cool, sparse and dense,\" and scores appearing on frames for \"a vision judge looks at every one.\" Then scene 5 could shift to the archive/Studio footage sped up to depict \"keeps the best in each region,\" filling the archive map by cell with best-per-cell badges before cutting to scene 6 — though I'm weighing whether the archive map is really the best way to convey that idea.\n\nFor timing, I'm mapping scene 4 (27.37–37.3s) to the develop source clip at 1x, which lines up perfectly, then scene 5 (37.3–45.03s, 7.73s) to the archive.webm footage showing a \"Cut the assets\" click followed by the archive filling at high speed ending on \"Finished.\" Working through the source segment math, I land on roughly 3.1x playback speed for the fill portion, which feels like a reasonable fast-forward — I'm considering adding a small \"×3\" speed indicator chip to keep it feeling like an honest lab recording rather than misleading the viewer.\n\nI'm checking whether the scroll-to-map moment at 13.1–13.8s source time would look like an awkward jump if sped up, and testing an alternative split where the click plays at 1x then cuts to the map-filling footage at roughly 2.83x — that cut from the form to the map view would need a quick crossfade to smooth the jump. I'm also weighing a simpler option of skipping the click entirely and starting right on the map fill at about 2.21x, but I lean toward keeping the click since it reinforces \"what it's like to use it\" even though the narration itself focuses more on results.\n\nFor scene 6, I'm laying out the archive.webm segment at 1x followed by the asset wall and tokens clips, but I notice archive.webm is used continuously across scenes 5 and 6 at different rates, so I need to check that the cut point between scenes lands cleanly on the source timeline.\n\nNow I'm shifting to the \"measure\" scene, which pulls persona data for Ren from the base-personas file, while the asset wall relies on fittings assets shared across all three personas. I should also check fal for a music model and its cost before finalizing, and before building anything I need to gather the logo SVG, Ren's persona data, run data, fittings and companions image dimensions, the tokens frame, and the deploy URL.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3688,"cache_read_input_tokens":160621,"output_tokens":18019,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":17823},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3688,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":18019,"cache_read_input_tokens":160621,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3688,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3688},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":182605,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"hyperframes-core","type":"assistant","uuid":"503336b0-c405-4ba0-a377-60a0a3f3d56d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:34:01.941Z","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"503336b0-c405-4ba0-a377-60a0a3f3d56d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuauZe9UnUpTXHfW5CNu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VNCNEk3Dd6U7WsCGNi8ugb","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat site/src/components/layout/baste-logo.tsx | head -60; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport re\ns=open('src/persona/base-personas.ts').read()\ni=s.find('nightmarketcoder')\nprint(s[i-200:i+3500])\nEOF","description":"Read the logo SVG and Ren's persona data"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3688,"cache_read_input_tokens":160621,"output_tokens":18019,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":17823},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3688,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":18019,"cache_read_input_tokens":160621,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3688,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3688},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VNCNEk3Dd6U7WsCGNi8ugb":{"command":"cd /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste && cat site/src/components/layout/baste-logo.tsx | head -60; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport re\ns=open('src/persona/base-personas.ts').read()\ni=s.find('nightmarketcoder')\nprint(s[i-200:i+3500])\nEOF","description":"Read the logo SVG and Ren's persona data"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01VNCNEk3Dd6U7WsCGNi8ugb":{"cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"hyperframes-core","type":"assistant","uuid":"83b8e133-ef75-46e4-9bfb-dfc7979069d0","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:34:01.958Z","effort":"max","perTur