{"parentUuid":"a0fca463-07de-469b-af91-88f1f4747a64","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfubAuebZzEqeJUpckzy9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I should give the user a quick status update before diving in. I'm planning the composition at 1920×1080 with narration totaling 69.33s, including the final \"yours\" scene lasting 5.43s starting at 63.9s, and considering extending the end card by about 2.5s so the total runs closer to 72s.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the scene-by-scene structure using the timing.json data — starting with the \"why\" scene from 0 to 8.73s, where the voiceover critiques generic demographic targeting (\"Most interfaces are cut for 'users aged twenty-five to thirty-four'... Nobody in that bracket has taste... A person does.\"). Visually I'm picturing a warm drafting-paper background with a grid, the Baste logo drawing in, and a sterile \"25-34, Urban, Tech-savvy\" persona card rendered in flat generic styling to contrast with the narration's point.\n\nI'm scripting the beats: the demographic tags appear first, then get struck through with red basting thread as the \"Nobody in that bracket has taste\" line lands, fading to low opacity, before resolving into a bold Bodoni headline like \"Not a bracket. A person.\" for the final beat — keeping onscreen text complementary to the spoken line rather than redundant. Then I'm moving into the \"measure\" scene starting at 8.73s with its own voice timing around 9.18 to 18.51s.\n\nFor this segment I'm syncing voiceover phrases to a \"measurement sheet\" visual — Ren's large portrait on the left labeled like a tailor's spec sheet, with four rows (films, music, rooms, dislikes) revealing in time with each spoken clause, each row ticking in like a tape measure. I'm also considering a quick flash of all three personas (Moss/Ren/Ada) at the opening line \"Baste starts with one person\" before settling on Ren as the featured subject.\n\nThen for the \"fitting\" segment, I'm timing the fitting.webm footage so the tab switches between Ren, Ada, and Moss line up with the narration about \"Same screen, three people\" and \"the layout, the type, the colour and the motion all change\" — trimming the clip from its start mark to around 16 seconds to capture those transitions.\n\nTrying 5.2 to 14.6 gives 9.4s source at 1.16× speed for 8.1s output -- I'd rather keep 1× playback for realism and just trim the segment instead.\n\nTaking the 5.4 to 13.5 window at natural speed gives 8.1s showing Moss briefly, then Ren, then ending on Ada, with cursor movements cueing each switch about 0.7s before it happens. I'm thinking about framing this footage inside a rounded browser window with a subtle shadow, sized to fit the 1920×1080 canvas while respecting the footage's aspect ratio.\n\nSettling on placing the footage at native 1440×900 scale for pixel crispness, centered near the top with a 40px margin, so it occupies most of the vertical space while leaving a bottom band free for burned-in captions — avoiding any overlap with the UI. That leaves 240px side margins, which I can use for a scene label like \"03 · Fitting.\"\n\nActually, captions belong at bottom center as a floating pill, and punching into UI regions (Ken Burns style) helps readability since the footage is already native resolution without needing browser chrome. I'm leaning toward a design with the footage window scaled slightly larger than source, centered on a paper ground, letting the recorded site nav serve as its own chrome.\n\nI'm sizing the bottom band at roughly 84px, enough for one line of 34px caption text chunked into phrase-level segments around 60 characters each, which fits comfortably within the frame width. I'm also considering placing a small chapter marker in the band or side margins to label sections without crowding the caption text.\n\nReconsidering scale though—going with 1.0 scale at 1440×900 positioned at x=240, y=40 gives a generous 140px caption band and 240px margins that frame the footage like a printed page. But I need to account for how UI text renders when the video shrinks down to fit the site at around 1000px wide, since small text at 13px could become illegible at that reduced scale.\n\nSo punch-ins become essential: for key moments like the archive grid or conversation views, I'll zoom 1.35–1.6× into the relevant region while keeping the window frame fixed and treating the footage as a viewport with overflow hidden, panning and scaling the inner content. I'm settling on a window at x=192, y=40, w=1536, h=960 with a 1.6 aspect ratio, base inner scale of 1.0667, and captions centered around y≈1040 using 32px Instrument Sans in an ink-on-paper style.\n\nFor scenes like \"why\", \"measure\", and the end card that are fully designed rather than footage-based, they'll use the full canvas with their own layout, but I'll keep the caption band consistent at the bottom.\n\nNow I'm timing the \"baste\" section (27.37→37.3s, voice 27.82→36.54s), breaking the voiceover into three phrases based on silence detection -- \"To find a direction,\" then \"Baste tries dozens of loose versions, warm and cool, sparse and dense,\" then \"and a vision judge looks at every one\" -- mapped to global timestamps around 27.82–36.08s, paired with the contact-sheet developing visual from method.webm.\n\nI'm deciding between method.webm and archive.webm for this scene, and archive.webm fits better since its map plots candidates by temperature and density, directly visualizing \"warm and cool, sparse and dense.\" Following that, the \"fit\" scene about keeping the best in each region rather than one winner could use either method.webm's judging/cut phase with loupe criteria, or archive.webm's final state with count badges, and then \"cut\" moves into the final usable outputs.\n\nNow I'm working out timing: archive.webm marks selecting the provider around 4.5s, clicking Cut at 11.86s, finishing near 30.98s, with the candidate-landing window spanning roughly 19 seconds against a 9.93s baste scene. I'm considering covering the provider click during the \"To find a direction\" narration, then compressing the 19-second fill into about 8 seconds at 2.4x speed so it still reads as \"dozens\" of candidates landing quickly, while also accounting for page scroll cuts around 13.1s.\n\nAlternatively, I'm testing a two-clip split — one near-real-time clip showing the click on \"Cut the assets,\" then a faster second clip covering the archive build-up — to land closer to the exact 9.93s target, possibly using hard cuts with brief crossfades instead of worrying about scene overlap. Looking at the contact sheet frames, I can pin down that the provider switches to \"fal,\" dry run gets unchecked, and the actual \"Cut the assets\" click happens right around the 11-12s mark.\n\nRefining the timing further: showing the dry-run untick with its cost estimate (~$1.47, 18 candidates) then the click at 1× takes about 3.5s, leaving the remaining archive-build footage to run at ~2.95× to hit 9.9s total — fast, but it reads as energetic rather than confusing. I'm also considering punching in on the archive map region as it fills, since that's where the visual interest is concentrated.\n\nFor the fit segment (37.3–45.033s), I'm mapping voice lines about keeping \"the best in each region, not one winner\" to the judging/cut phase of the method footage, pulling specific frames showing the loupe appearing over the brief and circles marking kept drafts.\n\nWorking out the timing math: starting the scroll at media 2.8s, the judge phase (0.62 mark) lands around 10.34s, and the scroll hits the 0.74 threshold—where the sheet with scores appears—at roughly 11.8s.\n\nThen a second scroll from 12.1 to 0.99 over 4s ease-out means the kept circles should surface around 12.4–13.5s with the loupe around 13.6s. I want those circles to appear on the kept drafts during the \"it keeps the best in each region\" narration, then transition to the archive map's final state showing multiple kept directions during the next line about seeing the real range of options.\n\nFor timing, I'm working out the media window for the archive clip — using roughly 9.9 to 16.6 seconds of source footage (6.7s), then crossfading for 1s into the next scene, with a playback rate around 0.87 to stretch it to fit the 7.73s scene duration.\n\nFor the method page punch-in, I'm calculating the film-strip sheet's position at full resolution (roughly x 540–1410, y 115–750) and planning a 1.25× zoom centered around (975, 430) to draw focus to the sheet before cutting to the next segment at 45.033 → 54.\n\nNow I'm parsing the silence detection output for the \"cut\" segment running 45.483 → 54.16 (about 9.9s), mapping out where the leading silence ends and the brief pauses fall between phrases to figure out natural break points for voice timing.\n\nWith those gaps, I'm matching up phrases to their timestamps: \"What comes out is ready to use:\" around 45.6–47.9, then \"images,\" \"vector icons,\" \"ambient loops,\" and \"design tokens for CSS, Tailwind or Panda\" filling out the rest of the segment up to roughly 53.74.\n\nFor the visuals, I'm picturing an asset wall built from the actual generated outputs — Ren's hero image, an icon, an icon collection, a short loop video, and footage of tokens switching between CSS, Tailwind, and Panda tabs — each labeled with a fit score. I'm debating whether showing the lower icon fit scores (0.35–0.55) undermines the pitch, versus just being honest since the site already displays them; I'll lean toward showing the strong hero/loop scores prominently.\n\nInstead of fit scores in this particular cut scene, I think cleaner is just labeling each asset with the model that produced it (Seedream v4, Recraft v3 vector, Seedance 1 Pro) in small mono type, since designers care about provenance. I'm sequencing this around the 45–48 second mark, using the archive clip's \"The cut · 5 assets\" moment with its thumbnails and download links shown inside the Studio, running about 3.2 seconds at normal speed.\n\nFrom the contact sheet, I can pin down the stable cut view to the 32.6–36.0 second range of that footage. Then at 48.2–50.4 I'll show a full asset wall with a large hero image plus icon and collection appearing, followed by the ambient loop playing in a tile around 50.7–51.7, and finally the design tokens footage for CSS, Tailwind, and Panda from 51.8–54.9, timing the tab clicks to match the narration.\n\nI should pin down exact tab-switch times before finalizing timing, and consider structuring the cut scene as the \"The cut\" studio footage mapped to archive media around 32.7–35.8s.\n\nMoving on to segment 7, the \"talk\" clip runs from 54.933s to 63.9s with voice starting at 55.383s, delivering the line about asking the persona why and what they love about design. The visual shows the cursor moving to the Ren tab, clicking into the Ren portrait, then continuing with further clicks.\n\nI'm mapping out the timing of a suggestion prompt (\"What are you into?\") around 5.5s into that clip, Ren's reply appearing between 7-11.6s, then a click on \"Why these colours?\" around 13-16.2s triggering a reply with palette swatches. I'm also cross-referencing scene-detection timestamps with a contact sheet breakdown — tracking frames of Moss switching to the Ren tab, Ren's greeting appearing, and the suggestion being sent.\n\nI'm working out the trim and speed rate for this scene, weighing whether to cut the tab-click transition and start right at Ren's greeting (10.6s duration, 1.18x rate) versus keeping it for a more natural flow, and matching it against the narration line about asking the persona why and how it describes what it loves.\n\nFor the framing, I'm thinking about zooming into the conversation panel but being careful not to crop out Ren's portrait, which feels important to the charm of the scene—leaning toward a subtle 1.12x zoom centered to keep both the chat and portrait visible, though I'll finalize that during the snapshot.\n\nMoving to the \"yours\" segment, spanning roughly 63.9 to 69.3 seconds plus an end card hold to about 73 seconds, with voice timing from 64.35 to 68.56. The spoken text breaks into phrases like \"Bring your own people,\" \"Measure them in the Studio,\" and \"and cut something that fits,\" though I need exact timestamps from silence detection since that output got truncated. Visually this pairs with the yours.webm footage showing typed examples like \"The Night Potter\" and a ceramicist persona glazing at 3am to Arthur Russell records.\n\nI'm working through the typing timing more carefully -- the name field types in about 1.5 seconds and the summary field takes longer due to per-character delays plus re-render lag, so actual typing runs closer to 5.1-6.6s for the name and 9.5-13.1s for the summary. For the 5.43 second scene leading into the end card, I'm thinking of showing the name being typed along with its auto-generated slug, running about 3 seconds before the end card appears around 66.9.\n\nI'm weighing options for pacing: two separate quick cuts (name then summary) feels choppy, so instead I'm considering playing the media from 4.6 to 13.2 seconds at 1.6× speed, which compresses it to about 5.4 seconds and still reads as a natural fast-typing pace. That way the end card can overlap with the tail end of the narration around 67.4, right as \"cut something that fits\" is spoken, with the narration itself ending at 68.56.\n\nI should check the README for install instructions or a package name, searching for \"npx\" or \"npm install\" references, and also figure out what's shown during the \"why\" scene of the title.\n\nFor the opening visual, I'm sketching a pattern-paper background with drafting grid lines and a dashed basting stitch, placing the Baste logo top-left, then showing a generic \"persona card\" mockup with a grey avatar silhouette and demographic fields to illustrate the line about interfaces being designed for narrow age ranges.\n\nAt that line, a red strike cuts through the \"25-34\" chip and the taste field goes blank, the generic card fading out. Then the card slides away and Ren's actual portrait appears in a tall frame with a pinned tag \"Ren · The night market\" in italic Bodoni, paired with the headline \"Design for one person\" leading into the measurement sheet scene.\n\nI'm thinking of making Ren's portrait its own persistent clip spanning the transition from why to measure, animating its position across the timeline so it doesn't get stuck inside one scene. For the measure scene, I'll restructure so that all three portraits—Moss, Ren, and Ada—fan in together as pinned swatch cards with name tags before the \"Baste starts with one person\" line.\n\nThen I'm designing the measurement sheet layout: Moss and Ada slide away while Ren enlarges on the left, and a right-side \"Measurement Sheet\" panel builds row by row with synced reveals—rewatched films, music influences, home environments, pet peeves—each row styled with mono uppercase labels and Bodoni values, underlined by a tick-mark tape-measure line. It wraps up with an aesthetic readout summarizing the vibe in a few descriptive words.\n\nNow I'm sketching the next scene, a fitting sequence using footage, framed inside a paper-grid window with rounded corners and soft shadow, scaled video inside, and a rotated chapter label like \"03 — FITTING\" running down the left margin in mono type.\n\nI also want to work out the captions band position across scenes, aligning its baseline just below the window's bottom edge consistently. For captions, I'm deciding to burn them in so autoplay works muted on the landing page, while still providing a VTT track for accessibility that stays off by default to avoid duplication.\n\nI'll settle on burned-in captions for accessibility plus a visible transcript below the video, and separately generate a chapters VTT with scene names for navigation without attaching it as a duplicate caption track. I'm also thinking about whether to add narration audio per scene and whether fal can generate background music to accompany the video.\n\nI'm also considering other fal music endpoints like Lyria 2, Minimax Music, Ace-Step, ElevenLabs Music, and CassetteAI, though I'm not fully certain which are live or their exact pricing and clip-length limits.\n\nSince I need roughly 73 seconds of audio, I'll try ElevenLabs music with a specific length parameter first, falling back to Stable Audio 2.5 if needed since it reportedly supports longer durations. I need to retrieve the fal API key from skate without ever exposing it in any artifact, and I'm planning to handle this via a one-off shell command rather than a persistent script to keep the key out of files.\n\nActually, mirroring the narrate-tour pattern with a reproducible script makes sense despite adding some repo surface. For the music itself, I'm drafting a prompt for a warm, understated lo-fi/chamber ambient bed — felt piano, brushed bass, light vinyl crackle, steady tempo, no drums or buildups, ending on a resolved chord to match the tailoring/atelier aesthetic, then I'll need to duck the music under the narration.\n\nI'm setting the voice volume to a constant low level with a slight rise and fade near the end card, using volume automation lanes. Checking fal's cost is fine since the budget used so far is modest and music generation is cheap, so I can proceed to think through the fitting-room scene transitions and the method page footage showing the persona tabs and draft counts.\n\nI'm weighing persona consistency across clips — Moss appears in the method recording with a richer draft sheet, while Ren shows up in the archive and fitting scenes. Since the narration never names anyone, I'll keep Moss's lush contact sheet for the method scene even though it breaks strict continuity with Ren's archive footage.\n\nChecking what Ren's final archive map actually shows at the end: it's sparse, with only the top row of the 5×5 grid filled since Ren's candidates are all dense/maximalist, giving an honest but visually thin \"3 directions kept\" outcome.\n\nFor pacing the fit, I'm thinking the method sheet should open on the \"judged\" state with score chips around the 10-second mark, then move into the circles-and-loupe sequence through about 16.7 seconds. After that comes the cut — \"5 assets\" from the studio archive, then the designed wall, then tokens. I also want the measure scene to use the stylized clay-like 3D portrait for Ren, and I'm reconsidering the bland persona card in the why scene to make sure it reads clearly as a parody.\n\nI'm picturing a grey-avatar \"Alex\" persona card with generic demographic rows (age, location, income, devices, goals) and a blank \"Taste\" field — when the line about nobody in that bracket having taste lands, that row underlines in red with a stitch tearing diagonally across the card, then it slides away as three real portrait cards take its place. For type, I'm pairing Bodoni Moda headlines with Instrument Sans body text and IBM Plex Mono labels. I'm second-guessing whether to put an on-screen headline during the why scene since it risks just repeating the bottom-caption narration rather than adding anything new.\n\nI'm leaning toward keeping big on-screen text minimal — letting the card itself do the talking — and introducing the three portraits with simple name/place tags (a greenhouse, a night market, a quiet archive) when \"a person does\" lands, plus a small persistent logo rather than a big opening title card since the cold open into the persona critique is more engaging. Now I'm deciding on a monolithic single-file structure for all eight scenes rather than splitting into modules, and starting to lay out the narration audio elements with their start-time data attributes.\n\nFor the music bed and footage clips I'm setting up muted autoplay video elements, keeping in mind the rule that a timed media element should never share a `data-start` ancestor — so the window-frame wrapper around each video clip stays untimed while its opacity gets animated separately, and the actual video tag inside carries the timing data.\n\nI'm setting scenes up as timed sections with class \"clip scene\" containing inner GSAP-animated nodes, keeping the .clip element itself static while animating its children. For the Ren portrait spanning the why-to-measure transition, I'm working out where the \"why\" scene should end.\n\nRather than fighting boundary continuity between \"why\" and \"measure,\" I'm merging them into one continuous \"intro\" clip (0–19.267s) holding all three portrait elements, since both are designed scenes anyway—this sidesteps position-matching issues at the 8.733s cut. I'm also thinking through how the \"cut\" scene layers designed wall elements against the window frame and footage.\n\nFor transitions, full-frame designed clips get an opaque paper background that sits over the window footage, and I'll crossfade by animating an inner wrapper's opacity rather than the clip itself. For captions, I'm planning a timed caption layer with phrase-level divs in the bottom band, fading via inner spans, with phrases derived from silence detection—I'll need to re-run that analysis for the remaining scenes.\n\nChecking other constraints: no `<br>` tags in body text, no randomness since GSAP timelines must stay deterministic, and fonts loading from local variable woff2 files with a weight range. I'm also verifying contrast ratios, like whether the red mono label color holds up against the paper background by computing its luminance components.\n\nThat gives ratio 5.35, which works well. Checking the grey text on the card too — #8b877f against the off-white card background only hits 3.37, failing the 4.5 threshold, so I'd switch to a darker #6b675f, which achieves 5.3 and passes.\n\nFor the layout, I'm skipping the rotated chapter label idea since there's no room above the window; instead I'll place a chapter index like \"03 · FITTING\" left-aligned in the caption band, with captions centered and a small logo/wordmark right-aligned — giving a consistent three-part lower band across the intro, wall, and other designed scenes.\n\nNow I'm working out the zoom/pan math per clip: since the footage is 1440×900 but the window is 1536×960, I need a base scale factor, then for any zoom level centered on a target point in the footage, I'm computing the scale and translation so that point lands at the window's center, with clamping to keep the content covering the window.\n\nI'm also being careful about a lint rule against mixing CSS initial transforms with GSAP tweens on the same property, so I'll keep the starting state set via GSAP rather than CSS, using a helper function to compute x/y/scale per zoom target and driving everything through the timeline. Transform-origin is a separate property so I can still set that in CSS safely.\n\nNow I'm checking video performance details -- the webm source runs at 1440×900 VP9 25fps, with ffmpeg handling frame extraction and proxying to H.264 if needed for preview. I'm pinning down the exact tab-switch timestamps from the scene detection (around 6.48s, 10.24s, 14.08s) and mapping them against the cursor's move/click timing so the click animation lands right before each content swap.\n\nFor the fitting scene spanning global time 19.267 to 27.367, I'm aligning narration beats (\"Then it fits an interface to them,\" \"Same screen, three people,\" \"The layout, the type, the colour and the motion all change\") with the Moss/Ren/Ada tab transitions. I'm testing a playback rate of 1.4 to figure out where each switch should land in global time, working through the math for when Ren and Ada should appear based on the media's original 6.48s and 10.24s swap points.\n\nI settle on a full cycle Moss → Ren → Ada → Moss at roughly 2.7s per persona, which gives a satisfying rhythm, and I decide the fitting room footage should stay mostly full-frame with a subtle push-in from 1.0 to 1.06 zoom across the scene.\n\nNow I'm scoping out the next archive scene, \"baste,\" running from 27.367 to 37.3 seconds, and starting to break it into two sub-clips with specific media timecodes and playback speeds.\n\nI'm narrowing the clip B pacing so it lands exactly at 37.3s, adjusting the rate slightly to close the 0.13s overage.\n\nNow I'm calculating the zoom crop for the archive grid, centering on its bounding box and clamping the x/y offsets so the frame stays within the right edge boundary.\n\nI'm also noting that around media time 13.1–13.7, the recorder scrolls the page to bring the archive map into view, which shifts the layout right as segment B begins.\n\nTo avoid showing that scroll mid-transition, I'll start B at media 14.0 instead, once the scroll has settled — at that point the log already shows \"Generation 1/5,\" so we lose a little of the initial candidate landing but gain a stable frame. That gives roughly 16.95s of source at rate 2.74 for a 6.183s duration.\n\nBetween A's end at 11.95 and B's start at 14.0, there's a jump since things are mid-cutting/progress state — a quick 0.25s crossfade should smooth that cut over. For the \"fit\" scene, I'm mapping the method clip (10.0 to 16.65s, 6.65s duration) at rate 0.86 into a 7.733s global span from 37.3 to 45.033, with a zoom centered on the sheet and loupe area at (975, 430), scale 1.2 — and I'm double-checking the method clip's actual duration (17.24s, ending mark at 16.71) to make sure the math holds. Then the \"cut\" scene spans globally from 45.033 to 54.933.\n\nBreaking down that 9.9s \"cut\" scene: C1 uses archive media from 32.7 to 35.9s at 1× speed mapping to global 45.033–48.233, syncing with the \"What comes out is ready to use\" line, and I need to extract a frame around media time 34 to nail down the exact layout coordinates for the \"cut · 5 assets\" zoom target in the 1440×900 frame. Then the designed asset wall section runs 48.233 to 51.8s, syncing hero image, icon SVGs, and loop video appearances with the \"images, vector icons, ambient loops\" narration beats. C2 then uses tokens media at ~1.6× rate for the final 3.133s, syncing with \"design tokens for CSS, Tailwind or Panda\" — though I still need exact tab-switch timestamps from the source frames. After that, the \"talk\" scene spans 54.933 to 63.9s, pulling from talk media 6.6 to 15.6s at roughly 1.0x rate.\n\nI'm working out the click timing for this talk sequence — the Moss→Ren switch happens around 5.48s, then after a 1.4s wait the cursor moves to click \"What are you into?\" around 7.7s, reply bubbles stagger in with typing delays finishing near 10.5s, then a 2.6s wait before marking the segment end around 11.63s, followed by a \"Why these colours?\" click near 12.6s with the palette reply landing between 14.3 and 15.5s. For the \"yours\" scene (global 63.9–67.6s), I'm mapping yours media from 8.9 to 13.2s at a 1.16× rate, estimating the name gets typed by ~6.6s and the summary typing runs roughly 9.5–13.1s, though I need to pull exact frames to confirm, along with the zoom target on the \"Who they are\" form section.\n\nNow I'm designing the end card: a 5.6s full-frame close with the Baste mark and italic Bodoni wordmark on the left, a headline reading \"Design for one person.\" with \"one person\" styled in italic red beneath it, a mono code chip showing a generate command plus an \"open the Studio\" prompt, and on the right a fanned stack of the three fitted hero cards (Moss, Ren, Ada). I'm timing the narration to end at 68.56s with the music swelling and fading out by 73.0s, setting the total root duration to 73.0s, and now moving on to pull the silence-detection data for the captions phrase list.\n\nSince exact timestamped text overlays are risky without proper font support, I'm planning to extract individual frames at specific candidate times for the archive, tokens, yours, and talk segments, then assemble them into tiled contact sheets I can review in order to pin down the right moments -- plus checking the cut view frame at archive media time 34.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":78083,"cache_read_input_tokens":91599,"output_tokens":22410,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":21810},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":78083,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":22410,"cache_read_input_tokens":91599,"cache_creation_input_tokens":78083,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":78083},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":228905,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"hyperframes-core","type":"assistant","uuid":"c2569e2f-1946-4116-89f6-ed199973fcf8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:38:19.647Z","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
