{"parentUuid":"bd879b0a-12ae-4c7a-adf2-877435c29dd4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"environment","snapshot":{"workingDirectory":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","isWorktree":false,"isGitRepo":true,"additionalWorkingDirectories":["/home/diogenes/.t3/userdata/attachments"],"platform":"linux","shell":"bash","osVersion":"Linux 6.19.14-108.fc42.x86_64"},"changes":[{"field":"workingDirectory","from":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c3392e73-5823-44d9-8328-8be9a256336a","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:45:47.756Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n# Environment update\n - Primary working directory: /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour (was /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste)\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"c3392e73-5823-44d9-8328-8be9a256336a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfuc3RMRVZkKkfetG3x4v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"With the word timings set, I'm now sketching a 1920×1080 composition lasting around 69.33 seconds, plus a short end-card hold of a couple seconds to extend the total runtime to roughly 72 seconds.\n\nFor the visual system I'm pulling from the Baste brand: cream paper background, dark ink text, red basting-stitch accents, blue link color, and a type system mixing Bodoni Moda, Instrument Sans, and IBM Plex Mono, plus the needle-and-dashed-stitch mark.\n\nFor scene one (\"why\"), I'm imagining a mock demographic spec label like \"Users aged 25–34\" rendered in mono as if it's a wireframe spec sheet, then struck through with a red basting stitch at the \"nobody has taste\" line, before the italic Bodoni line \"A person does\" fades in — saving the portrait imagery for scene two.\n\nThen I'm moving into scene two (\"measure\"), which opens on the line about how Baste starts with something.\n\nFor visuals I'm picturing three portraits (Moss, Ren, Ada) narrowing to focus on Ren, with a measurement-sheet motif: rows populating in sync with the narration — films she rewatches, the music genres she works to, the late-night rooms she feels at home in, and finally the pet peeves she can't stand in an app.\n\nNext, moving into the \"fitting\" section (19.27–27.37), I'm thinking about how the same interface reshapes itself for three different people — adjusting layout, type, colour, and motion — paired with footage from fitting.webm.\n\nI'm testing different playback rates to align the tab-click intervals (3.76s apart in source) with the desired caption timing—trying 1.88x felt too fast for the cursor motion, so I'm checking 1.5x instead, which spaces switches at roughly 20.2, 22.7, and 25.2s.\n\nMapping backward from the 20.2s target, the source start would need to be around 5.085s, but at 1.5x speed the clip would run past the 16.92s source length by the end—overshooting slightly. I'm considering starting the clip a touch later with a crossfade transition from the previous scene to absorb the mismatch.\n\nTrying 1.6x speed instead maps the switch worse, overshooting even more. I'm also considering a simpler option: trimming the scene to only cover Moss → Ren → Ada without the third switch back to Moss, which gives more flexibility at 1.25x speed to align the timing cleanly.\n\nActually, the click transition itself matters — the garment re-cutting animation on persona change is the real star of this UI, so I shouldn't cut around it. Let me try a 1.45x playback rate instead, spanning the footage from the scene transition overlap through to source time ~16.4, which covers roughly 8.9 seconds of source material in under 13 seconds.\n\nMapping the persona switches to word timing: Ren's switch lands near \"them,\" Ada's near \"layout,\" and Moss's right after \"change\" — that lines up reasonably well with the dialogue cadence. I'm also factoring in the cursor's movement time to the tab (around 600-700ms) plus a brief pause and click before each transition fires.\n\nOn framing, the source footage is 1440×900 but the canvas is 1920×1080, so I'm thinking of scaling it to 1728×1080 with margin on the sides, or instead placing it inside a framed browser-style window (~1600×1000) with a soft shadow against the paper background for a cleaner product-video feel. That framing would also let me do subtle zoom/pan moves into the UI by scaling the wrapper, and I could add small mono chapter labels to mark each persona segment.\n\nNow I'm working out caption placement — if I burn captions at the bottom, I need to leave room below the frame or overlay them on a semi-opaque pill, though that risks covering parts of the UI, so I need to pick a safer position.\n\nActually I should simplify into two layout modes: \"Card\" scenes with full-canvas typographic compositions, versus \"Screen\" scenes where footage sits in a window with a chapter tag and title on the left and captions below. For the screen mode, I'm working out window dimensions scaled to about 0.9556, landing on roughly 1376×860 plus the title bar, positioned toward the right side of the canvas.\n\nI'm reconsidering the caption placement—rather than a plain bottom bar, I like the idea of the left column showing the narration as running text with a karaoke-style word highlight as it's spoken, which doubles as accessible captions while feeling more editorial and designed. I'm checking whether a 344px column at 30px type can comfortably fit the typical narration line lengths.\n\nFor consistency across scenes, I'm thinking the left rail could persist throughout—chapter tag plus spoken-text highlighting—while the right side alternates between footage and designed cards, forming a cohesive \"spec sheet\" layout with a fixed script rail and a flexible stage.\n\nThe opening and closing moments deserve full-canvas treatment instead: scene 1 (why) becomes kinetic typography synced to the words themselves, making captions inherent to the design, and the end card similarly uses large display text like \"Bring your own people.\" The middle scenes keep the rail-plus-stage structure, with the stage showing either composed HTML cards (like the measurement sheet) or footage (assets wall, tokens).\n\nFor the finale, I'll start with the \"yours\" footage of Studio's persona-typing flow before transitioning to the end card with the Baste logo and tagline. Since there's no confirmed public URL, I'll show the CLI command `baste gui` as the call to action instead, since that's what the Studio explicitly tells users to run.\n\nFor the \"baste\" scene (27.37–37.30), I'm mapping it to the archive footage: scrolling to the title, selecting the provider, clicking \"run\" around 11.86, watching the archive map populate through candidates landing near 30.9, then cutting at 32.59. The run segment is nearly 19 seconds of source footage, which at 2x speed compresses to about 9.5 seconds, so I'm weighing which beats to keep—likely trimming to the provider selection, unticking Dry run, and clicking \"Cut the assets.\"\n\nFor the fit scene, I'm pairing it with method.webm footage, planning to show the click around 11.2 seconds then the archive filling in through the results, aiming to land scenes 4 and 5 at roughly 9.93 and 7.73 seconds respectively.\n\nI'm thinking the archive fill could speed up to around 2.2x after the 12-second mark to compress the 13-to-31 range into about 9 seconds, and the \"warm and cool, sparse and dense\" narration could be paired with animated arrow overlays tracing the map's temperature and density axes. For the \"vision judge looks at every one\" line, I'm considering a loupe or highlight overlay to visualize the judging moment.\n\nFor scene 5 on fitness, I want to use the method.webm footage showing the contact sheet with circled kept frames and the judge's loupe notes — pulling source from around 7.5 to 16.7 seconds and speeding it to about 1.2x so it lands at the scene's 7.73-second target, timing it to the \"keeps the best in each region\" line.\n\nAlternatively, I'm weighing archive.webm's \"done to cut\" segment (30.98–36.81), which shows the studio's archive results with the cut assets list and fit scores — though that content might fit scene 6's \"ready to use\" beat better than scene 5.\n\nScene 6 (45.03–54.93) walks through what comes out: images, vector icons, ambient loops, and design tokens for CSS, Tailwind, or Panda. I'm planning the first half as an \"asset wall\" showing the actual generated outputs for the three personas — hero images, SVG icons, and looping ambient audio clips — arranged in a designed grid that labels each category as it appears.\n\nFor the second half, I'm checking the tokens.webm footage to pin down exactly when the tabs switch between CSS variables, Tailwind, and Panda, since the scene-detect cuts were too subtle to rely on. Working frame-by-frame from the timestamp sheet instead to figure out roughly when each tab gets clicked and highlighted.\n\nThat interval gives too fast a playback rate at 4.2x, so I'm trying other timing options — like placing the clicks at roughly 51.3 and 53.0 seconds, giving a 1.7s gap and a 1.8x rate instead, though that's not quite satisfying either.\n\nThinking about using hard cuts or crossfades between sub-clips for CSS variables, Tailwind, and Panda, zoomed into the code panel so each flip syncs with the spoken word \"CSS, Tailwind, or Panda\" — that feels intentional and readable. Actually, it might be simpler and more reliable to just render the token code myself in HTML rather than relying on captured footage timing.\n\nReal footage is better for showing \"what it's like to use it,\" but zooming cropped footage 1.6x would make the small code text blurry — acceptable for a few seconds, but not ideal. A cleaner alternative is keeping the footage at normal window size and adding an overlay chip row reading \"CSS · Tailwind · Panda\" that lights up with each spoken word, letting the tabs switch naturally in the background while staying legible.\n\nTrying to time the clip sync: starting the tokens footage at source 3.6s with a 1.6x playback rate over ~4.4s, the Panda chip lights up almost perfectly at the right moment, but Tailwind appears about 1.25s too early relative to the spoken word. I'm adjusting the start point to fix that mismatch.\n\nThen I piece together where footage B picks up at the Panda click, making the cut between A and B essentially a jump cut on the same screen where only the tab changes—barely noticeable since the cursor positions are close together. I still need the exact click timestamps in tokens.webm, so I'll detect frame differences in the tab region using ffmpeg crop and a lower scene-detection threshold, then move on to scene 7, the talk segment running from 54.93 to 63.90 seconds.\n\nFor talk.webm, I'm mapping out the click sequence: selecting Ren's tab, waiting, clicking the first suggestion, letting the reply stream in, then clicking a follow-up question before the clip ends. I'm cross-referencing scene detection timestamps against the frame sheet to pin down exactly when Ren gets selected and when each question gets clicked.\n\nThe source spans 4.0 to 16.2 seconds, and at 1.35x speed this maps to roughly 54.9–64.0 seconds — speeding up the typing feels natural since it's a chat exchange. I'm now moving on to the next scene, extending its duration slightly past 69 seconds to around 73.\n\nFor the final scene, I'm thinking through the voiceover beats — \"bring your own people,\" \"measure them in the Studio,\" \"cut something that fits\" — timed against footage of the persona form being filled out and summary typing, playing at 1.5x before transitioning into an end card with the Baste logo, the tagline, and the CTA showing the `baste gui` command alongside \"Studio · Method · Materials.\"\n\nNow I'm considering a richer end card design: pattern paper background, a large Baste mark with a needle stitching red dashed thread, the wordmark in Bodoni italic, and a tagline about interfaces fitted to one person, paired with a mono command chip and a \"Start your own fitting\" CTA. I'm also wondering whether background music fits the brief, since the voice-only question is still unresolved.\n\nI should re-read BRIEF.md and SCRIPT.md in full to settle these details. For transitions, I'm thinking a \"basting stitch wipe\" — a red dashed line sweeping across to reveal the next scene — would work well at major chapter changes, with simple crossfades elsewhere. I also want a persistent left rail showing the chapter index (Why, Measure, Fitting, Baste, Fit, Cut, Ask, Yours) in mono type with the current chapter highlighted, like a pattern piece list that visually tracks the flow.\n\nBelow that, the rail holds the narration as word-by-word highlighted captions — I'm working out the exact layout math: a 400px rail, footage window spanning x 464–1840 with an 80px right margin, and a vertical range of y 93–987 to center the 894px-tall content. The rail's top holds the wordmark and chapter list; the narration paragraph sits below using Instrument Sans at 28-30px with 1.35 line-height. Unspoken words appear faded while spoken words turn to full ink color, and I'm considering giving key terms a red dashed underline as they're spoken for a subtle stitching motif.\n\nFor accessibility I want to export a VTT caption file alongside the burned-in rail text, and I'm checking consistency across scenes — card scenes use kinetic on-screen type while the measure scene keeps the rail. I decide showing the full sentence greyed out ahead of time is fine, like a teleprompter karaoke effect common in explainer videos. For the footage window itself, I'm settling on rounded corners, a subtle border and shadow, and a minimal top bar with a studio label in mono type plus a thin red basting stitch line instead of the clichéd macOS dots.\n\nRather than a generic URL, I want the bar to show a breadcrumb-style label per scene like \"Landing · The fitting room\" or \"Studio · Generate\" — informative and on-brand. For motion, subtle slow push-ins on footage windows add life, and I'm thinking about zooming into the archive map specifically during the \"warm and cool, sparse and dense\" line to highlight the axes.\n\nI'm working out the exact timing and transform for that map zoom — scaling by 1.3 around the map's center point in window coordinates, held from roughly 30.5 to 35.5 seconds once the scroll settles.\n\nThe axis labels on the map (\"COOL — WARM\", \"SPARSE — DENSE\") already exist in the UI, so zooming reveals them naturally. I'm thinking about adding grease-pencil style callouts: an animated double-arrow along the color-temperature axis, one along the density axis, and a pointer to a specific judged entry like \"Candidate 18 judged 0.89\" to highlight how the vision judge evaluates each one.\n\nFor scene 2, the measurement sheet, I'm sketching how the stage area transitions: three portrait cards (Moss, Ren, Ada) start visible, then as the narration singles out \"one person,\" Moss and Ada slide out while Ren's card enlarges and shifts left, making room for a measurement sheet that reveals rows one at a time — films they rewatch, music, rooms, things they can't stand — each timed to specific narration cues.\n\nI'm thinking about how to render each row's values, maybe as pill-shaped tags or separated by dots, alongside a tailor's measuring tape graphic with tick marks to stay on-brand with \"measure.\" At the end of the sequence, the sheet condenses into an aesthetic profile — warm, maximalist, snappy, expressive — which bridges into the idea of fitting an interface to a person, the core concept of Baste.\n\nNow I'm shifting to scene 1, the \"why\" scene, planning the canvas background as cream-colored pattern paper with a faint grid, darker lines every fifth interval.\n\nIn the opening beats, I'm sequencing the kinetic text \"Most interfaces are cut for\" appearing word by word in large Bodoni, followed by a clothing size tag sliding in with \"USERS AGED 25–34\" in mono caps, rendered as a generic grey label with a hole and string. Then the tag gets struck through with a red basting stitch and desaturates as the line \"Nobody in that bracket has taste\" lands, before cutting to bold Bodoni text reading \"A person does,\" with \"person\" italicized in red — leaving room for Ren, Moss, and Ada's portraits to appear in the next scene. I'm also considering placing the Baste logo small in the top-left throughout, or opening with it front and center before the text begins.\n\nNow I'm planning a stitch-wipe transition where a red dashed line sweeps across to reveal the second scene's rail layout sliding in from the left as the headline shrinks. For the asset wall scene, I'm laying out a grid of actual outputs — a row of three hero images for Moss, Ren, and Ada labeled with their fit scores, a row of icon SVG tiles, and a row of looping ambient videos — before crossfading into footage of token chips for CSS, Tailwind, and Panda lighting up.\n\nI'm using short muted `<video>` elements with start/duration data for the loops since they only need to play a few seconds within the cut scene, so looping isn't necessary. I'm also cross-referencing the exact timing beats for when each asset type appears — images, icons, loops, then tokens with their CSS/Tailwind/Panda labels — and tying that to the archive footage showing a results list with fit scores and download links for the \"ready to use\" moment.\n\nI'm weighing whether to open scene 6 with a brief 2.3s shot of the Studio's cut results view (since it isn't shown elsewhere) before transitioning into the full asset wall for the remaining time, concluding that three visuals across roughly 10 seconds still reads as energetic rather than cluttered for this kind of product reel.\n\nFor audio, I'm deciding between separate narration clips per scene versus one mixed track, leaning toward per-scene audio elements for easier editing. I also need to check the brief for music requirements and consider generating a subtle background music bed via one of the available audio generation tools.\n\nI'm leaning toward ElevenLabs music on fal since we already use their TTS, with a prompt and music length parameter, but I should verify the exact schema before relying on it.\n\nBefore going further, I should check BRIEF.md and SCRIPT.md in full since they appeared compressed in the earlier output.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1779,"cache_read_input_tokens":191511,"output_tokens":13861,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":13712},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1779,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":13861,"cache_read_input_tokens":191511,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1779,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1779},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":145213,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"hyperframes-core","type":"assistant","uuid":"d914c996-0ec7-4f40-b24a-1f0bbd12954e","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:48:18.424Z","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"d914c996-0ec7-4f40-b24a-1f0bbd12954e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfuc3RMRVZkKkfetG3x4v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01E84WypbwdskwBrT51gttpa","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat BRIEF.md; echo ------; cat SCRIPT.md; echo -----; cat AGENTS.md | head -60","description":"Read the brief and script in full"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1779,"cache_read_input_tokens":191511,"output_tokens":13861,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":13712},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1779,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":13861,"cache_read_input_tokens":191511,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1779,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1779},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01E84WypbwdskwBrT51gttpa":{"command":"cd /home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour && cat BRIEF.md; echo ------; cat SCRIPT.md; echo -----; cat AGENTS.md | head -60","description":"Read the brief and script in full"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01E84WypbwdskwBrT51gttpa":{"cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"hyperframes-core","type":"assistant","uuid":"a79bc1c7-3dcb-4a46-b297-8626d110765a","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:48:18.438Z","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"a79bc1c7-3dcb-4a46-b297-8626d110765a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a21b9370-2685-47ed-b11e-b7c9dc33e565","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E84WypbwdskwBrT51gttpa","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nworkflow: product-launch-video\nflow: autonomous\nstoryboard: false\nmessage: \"Design for the person, not the persona document — Baste turns one person's taste into a whole interface, and shows you the spread of options it found.\"\ndestination: website (Baste landing page and Studio guide)\naspect: 1920x1080\nlanguage: en\nlength: 90s\nangle: product demo\nvoice: fal-ai/elevenlabs/tts/multilingual-v2, Aria (matches the existing Studio guide narration)\n---\n\n## Intent\n\nShow designers and developers what using Baste is like and why they'd use it.\nThe user asked for \"full demonstrations of the wonderful abilities of baste.\nwhat it is like to use it and why you would\", with generation done on fal.\nWarm, particular and confident — a tailor's atelier, not a SaaS explainer.\n\n## Story\n\n1. **Why.** Interfaces are cut for \"users aged 25–34\", who have no taste. A person does.\n2. **Measure.** A persona is films, music, rooms, tools and pet peeves — not demographics.\n3. **The fitting.** The same screen re-cut for Moss, Ren and Ada: layout, type, colour, motion.\n4. **Baste.** A real fal run: 24 candidates generated and judged by a vision model that looks at every image; the archive keeps the best in each region of colour temperature × density.\n5. **Fit.** The judge's weights: persona alignment 30, quality 20, uniqueness 20, coherence 15, usability 15.\n6. **Cut.** The finished suite: hero, background, Recraft vector icons, ambient loops, tokens as CSS/Tailwind/Panda/JSON.\n7. **Talk.** Ask the persona why — the conversation explains their taste in their own voice.\n8. **Make your own.** Studio, `npx baste gui`, `--provider fal`.\n\n## Assets\n\n- Real Studio and site screen recordings captured from http://127.0.0.1:5173 (landing, /gui, replayed recorded run).\n- Real generated outputs: `site/public/assets/fittings/` (heroes, backgrounds, icons, loops) and `site/public/assets/fittings/runs/*.json` (all 72 judged candidates with scores).\n- Companion portraits: `site/public/assets/companions/*.webp`.\n- House style: pattern paper #F1ECE2, ink #1C1B19, thread #D2402A, chalk #2F55A4, tape #F0C534; Bodoni Moda, Instrument Sans, IBM Plex Mono.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Everything shown is real output from this repository; no mocked UI and no invented capabilities.\n- Personas are authored characters, never presented as real people.\n- Captions in English (burned in for muted autoplay) and a WebVTT track.\n------\n# Baste tour — narration\n\nVoice: fal-ai/elevenlabs/tts/multilingual-v2, Aria, stability 0.6, similarity 0.75.\nOne line per scene; each scene holds until its line ends plus a short breath.\n\n| # | id | scene | line |\n|---|----|-------|------|\n| 1 | why | Title over pattern paper | Most interfaces are cut for “users aged twenty-five to thirty-four.” Nobody in that bracket has taste. A person does. |\n| 2 | measure | Three portraits, their influences | Baste starts with one person: the films they rewatch, the music they work to, the rooms they feel at home in, and the things they can’t stand in an app. |\n| 3 | fitting | The same screen re-cut for Moss, Ren and Ada | Then it fits an interface to them. Same screen, three people. The layout, the type, the colour and the motion all change. |\n| 4 | baste | The archive filling with real candidates | To find a direction, Baste tries dozens of loose versions, warm and cool, sparse and dense, and a vision judge looks at every one. |\n| 5 | fit | Judge weights; the kept variants | It keeps the best in each region, not one winner. So you see the real range of options before anything is final. |\n| 6 | cut | Finished suite: hero, icons, loop, tokens | What comes out is ready to use: images, vector icons, ambient loops, and design tokens for CSS, Tailwind or Panda. |\n| 7 | talk | Conversation with Ren | And you can ask the persona why. They’ll tell you, in their own words, what they love and what would make a design feel like theirs. |\n| 8 | yours | Studio, command, end card | Bring your own people. Measure them in the Studio, and cut something that fits. |\n-----\n# HyperFrames Composition Project\n\n## Skills — USE THESE FIRST\n\n**Always invoke the relevant skill before writing or modifying compositions.** Skills encode framework-specific patterns (e.g., `window.__timelines` registration, `data-*` attribute semantics, shader-compatible CSS rules) that are NOT in generic web docs. Skipping them produces broken compositions.\n\n**Doing anything with HyperFrames?** Start at `/hyperframes` — it tells you what HyperFrames can do and which skill or workflow handles your intent (make a video, TTS / BGM, prep footage, author / animate, render, install blocks), confirms your brief up front (the intent layer), and routes every \"make me a…\" request (a video, a deck, a composition port) to the right workflow. Read it first, especially when there's no project context to orient you. The workflows it routes to:\n\n- `/product-launch-video` — any **website** URL or brief / script → a product launch / SaaS / promo video, or a site tour / showcase featuring the site's own captured visuals.\n- `/faceless-explainer` — arbitrary text (topic / article / notes), **no URL, no website capture** → 60-90s faceless explainer.\n- `/embedded-captions` — an existing talking-head video (MP4) → the same footage with captions / subtitles added (rail + embed, or pure-cinematic embed); the footage itself is untouched.\n- `/talking-head-recut` — an existing talking-head / interview / podcast video (MP4) → the same footage **packaged with designed graphic overlays** (kinetic titles, lower-thirds, data callouts, pull-quotes, side panels, pip) synced to the transcript; the clip plays unchanged underneath. (Plain captions/subtitles → `/embedded-captions`.)\n- `/pr-to-video` — a GitHub PR (URL / `owner/repo#N` / \"this PR\") → 30-90s code-change explainer (changelog / feature reveal / fix / refactor).\n- `/motion-graphics` — a short (typically under 10s) design-led **motion graphic**, motion-is-the-message, no narration: kinetic type, a stat / number count-up, a chart, a logo sting, a lower-third / overlay, or an animated tweet / headline / captured-page highlight; rendered to MP4 or a transparent overlay. Longer / narrated / custom → `/general-video`.\n- `/music-to-video` — a **music track** (audio file, video to pull audio from, or one generated from a mood brief) → beat-synced video (lyric / slideshow / kinetic promo). Music drives pacing; user-supplied images / videos are cut onto the same beat grid.\n- `/slideshow` — a **presentation / pitch deck / interactive deck** — discrete slides, fragment reveals, branching, hotspot navigation, presenter mode. Output is a navigable deck, not a rendered video.\n- `/general-video` — fallback for any other video (title card, longer brand / sizzle reel, multi-scene montage, static loop, custom composition) and the home of **companion mode** — co-create with the full HyperFrames toolbox; the original hyperframes authoring flow, any length.\n\n**Porting an existing composition?** `/remotion-to-hyperframes` translates a Remotion (React) composition into HyperFrames HTML — a source migration, separate from the creation workflows above.\n\nThe domain skills (`/hyperframes-core`, `/hyperframes-animation`, `/hyperframes-keyframes`, `/hyperframes-creative`, `/hyperframes-cli`, `/media-use`, `/hyperframes-audio`, `/hyperframes-registry`, `/figma`) and the full capability map live inside `/hyperframes` — it is the single source of truth for which skill handles which intent.\n\n**Changing how real footage or images look or reveal?** Load `/media-use` and read its `references/media-treatments.md` before editing, even when the request only says dark, flat, boring, retro, private, or “make the reveal cooler.” It governs how footage is treated, never whether media may be used. Use canonical media treatments and seek-safe motion; do not improvise equivalent CSS/SVG filters or overlays.\n\n> **Tailwind v4 projects** (`hyperframes init --tailwind`): see `/hyperframes-core` → `references/tailwind.md`.\n\n> **Using a HyperFrames plugin?** Load skills from that installed bundle and follow\n> its `hyperframes/references/plugin-installation.md` execution rules. Update via\n> the plugin manager, not the standalone commands below.\n>\n> **Standalone skill missing or stale?** Run `npx hyperframes skills update <name>` to install/refresh\n> the specific skill you need (the `/hyperframes` router does this automatically before\n> entering a workflow), or bare `npx hyperframes skills update` to refresh the core set plus\n> everything already installed — neither pulls the full set. Restart the agent session so\n> newly installed skills load.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev          # human-operated foreground preview (blocks until stopped)\nnpx hyperframes preview --background  # agent-safe persistent Studio preview\nnpx hyperframes preview --status      # verify the persistent preview is listening\nnpx hyperframes preview --stop        # stop it when review is finished\nnpm run check        # lint + runtime + layout + motion + contrast (one command)\nnpm run render       # render to MP4\nnpm run publish      # publish and get a shareable link\nnpx hyperframes lint --verbose  # include info-level findings\nnpx hyperframes lint --json     # machine-readable output for CI\nnpx hyperframes docs <topic> # reference docs in terminal\n```\n\n> **Agents must use `npx hyperframes preview --background` for Studio handoff.** Do not rely\n> on a shell/tool `run_in_background` wrapper around `npm run dev`: that foreground process\n> remains owned by the invoking session and can disappear while the browser stays open,\n> leaving refreshes at `ERR_CONNECTION_TIMED_OUT`. Verify with `preview --status`, keep it\n> alive through review, and stop it explicitly with `preview --stop` afterward.\n\n> **Back from the desktop app.** Once this project was opened in the HyperFrames desktop app, run `npx hyperframes catch-up` before your next change here: it lists what the person asked Framey in the app and which files changed since.\n\n> **Pinned CLI version.** These scripts pin an exact `hyperframes@X.Y.Z` so this project re-renders identically over time. Weeks later that pin lags fixes shipped since. To move up: `npx hyperframes@latest upgrade --project . --check` (shows the delta), then `npx hyperframes@latest upgrade --project .` to rewrite the pins. Always unpinned — the pinned script re-runs the old version against itself.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"58c637a3-2cca-4bc6-9702-f64a78014d0e","timestamp":"2026-10-11T01:48:18.511Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nworkflow: product-launch-video\nflow: autonomous\nstoryboard: false\nmessage: \"Design for the person, not the persona document — Baste turns one person's taste into a whole interface, and shows you the spread of options it found.\"\ndestination: website (Baste landing page and Studio guide)\naspect: 1920x1080\nlanguage: en\nlength: 90s\nangle: product demo\nvoice: fal-ai/elevenlabs/tts/multilingual-v2, Aria (matches the existing Studio guide narration)\n---\n\n## Intent\n\nShow designers and developers what using Baste is like and why they'd use it.\nThe user asked for \"full demonstrations of the wonderful abilities of baste.\nwhat it is like to use it and why you would\", with generation done on fal.\nWarm, particular and confident — a tailor's atelier, not a SaaS explainer.\n\n## Story\n\n1. **Why.** Interfaces are cut for \"users aged 25–34\", who have no taste. A person does.\n2. **Measure.** A persona is films, music, rooms, tools and pet peeves — not demographics.\n3. **The fitting.** The same screen re-cut for Moss, Ren and Ada: layout, type, colo